                "text": "**Analyzing the Grid Structure**\n\nOkay, I've got a handle on the input grid's dimensions; it's a 12x9 arrangement. I'm noting the presence of \"8\" as a common value, and the distribution of zeros. Rows seem to have distinct patterns, such as sequences of \"8s\" interspersed with zeros. Row 3 specifically has a single \"8\".\n\n\n**Investigating the Gravity Concept**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on a gravity-based model. It seems that \"8\" values are \"falling\" downwards, filling up from the bottom. I've confirmed that the total number of \"8s\" remains constant between input and output, which fits this hypothesis. Specifically, I'm examining how the \"8s\" pass through holes in upper rows. I'm seeing an hourglass effect, with the sand accumulating at the bottom, and filling up fully from the bottom rows first.\n\n\n**Examining the Sand Piles**\n\nI'm now focusing on how the \"8\" values behave after encountering a gap. I've been looking at cases where a column contains only \"7\"s. It seems the \"8\"s split up and fall to fill up the left and right sides separately, based on their column. The 8s seem to just fall and add up to the bottom of where they came from.\n\n\n\n**Confirming the Hole Behavior**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on cases with holes formed by the \"7\" values. It seems the \"8\"s are definitely falling through these holes, and filling from the bottom up. I've confirmed this with multiple examples. The falling \"8s\" seem to distribute, splitting into left and right, and always preserving their total count. Row 5 has 7s at cols 0,1,2 and 5,6,7. Cols 3,4 are open, the sand, in this case, \"8s\", fall through the hole.\n\n\n**Determining the Distribution**\n\nI'm now investigating the placement of the \"8\" values after they fall. It appears that the distribution within a column with \"7\"s depends on the width of the open space above the \"7\"s. I'm noting examples where \"8\" values are centered within the available space after passing through a hole, accounting for the empty columns on both sides. The quantity of \"8\"s seems to follow a specific pattern relative to this open width.\n\n\n**Refining the Centering Rule**\n\nI'm revisiting the distribution mechanism for \"8\" values after they fall through a hole. I'm focusing on the centering logic when the open space's width doesn't perfectly accommodate the \"8\"s. I've noted a consistent left-favoring bias in the output when the remaining space is odd after centering. I'm verifying this with more examples.\n\n\n**Calculating the Sand Fall**\n\nI'm now determining how the \"8\" values fall through a large hole. I'm focusing on a specific case with 32 \"8\"s and a row of \"0\"s, such as row 4. I am calculating how many rows will be completely filled when all \"8\" values have fallen to the bottom, and then where any remaining \"8\"s will land. It seems I am starting to find a pattern on how to calculate the remainder.\n\n\n",
